mmc: dw_mmc: survive a card that stops responding mid-transfer

Two independent problems, both triggered on a Gusgu H7 (RK3326) when the RK915
sdio wifi chip drops off the bus under concurrent load.

1. TXDR/RXDR are only meaningful for PIO. dw_mci_submit_data() enables them on
   the PIO path, right after setting host->sg, and the DMA path deliberately does
   not - but the initial INTMASK enables them unconditionally and nothing ever
   turns them off, so they also fire throughout every DMA transfer where
   host->sg is NULL and the handler can only clear the latch. Harmless while the
   DMA drains the FIFO; not harmless when the card dies, because the FIFO
   condition then persists and the level-triggered line storms. Measured at
   ~684,000 interrupts/second, with every other interrupt on that CPU frozen:

       IRQTRACE 344 dwmci: 56:164736147  vop=11531 adc=24734 ser=418 gpio=10946
       IRQTRACE 345 dwmci: 56:165433415  vop=11531 adc=24734 ser=418 gpio=10946

   All device interrupts on this board are affine to CPU0, so the VOP stops (drm
   vblank timeouts), the SARADC stops (joypad error spam) and the network dies -
   the machine has to be power cycled. Mask them for the duration of a DMA
   transfer; the PIO path re-enables them when it needs them.

2. The software data timeout is armed for reads only. A write to a card that has
   stopped responding therefore has no timeout at all - the hardware data timeout
   does not cover the write data phase - so mmc_wait_for_req() waits forever:

       task:rk915_tx  state:D
         wait_for_completion / mmc_wait_for_req_done / mmc_io_rw_extended
         / sdio_memcpy_toio / rk915_data_write [rk915] / tx_thread [rk915]
       task:rk915_rx  state:D
         __mmc_claim_host / sdio_claim_host / rk915_serias_read [rk915]

   The rx thread is simply queued behind the tx thread on the host lock. The
   driver never sees an error, so its firmware recovery never runs, and the
   module cannot be unloaded. Arm dw_mci_set_drto() for writes as well. The
   timeout it computes comes from TMOUT, which dw_mci_set_data_timeout()
   programs per request from data->timeout_ns, so it is the timeout the core
   asked for rather than an arbitrary one.

Also included, found earlier in the same investigation: dw_mci_wait_while_busy()
would spin its full 500ms atomic poll on an all-ones SDMMC_STATUS read, and
dw_mci_interrupt() would service all 32 bits of an all-ones MINTSTS. Neither
value is data; bail out instead.

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 19:57:40.515908848 -0400
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 19:57:40.516985159 -0400
